          "question": "Burning when I pee and going more often since yesterday, no temperature.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in its red-flag table. That is not a clearance. If your symptoms change, get medical advice.\n\n**Seek same-day care or urgent help if any of these appear** (from the urinary tract infection reference):\n- Fever, chills, or shaking\n- Pain in your side or back (flank pain)\n- Nausea or vomiting, or feeling generally unwell\n- Blood in your urine. The reference says this is not by itself a sign of something worse, but it should be followed up.\n\nFor anyone over about 65, confusion, a fall, or a sudden drop in how well they function can be the main sign of infection, even without burning or fever. Do not wait for a fever to settle overnight if any warning sign appears. A normal temperature is not reassuring on its own. If you feel very unwell, seek emergency care.\n\n**What this pattern points to, by body system:**\n- **Urinary system (main focus):** Burning on passing urine and needing to go more often are the usual features of a lower urinary tract infection, according to the urinary tract infection reference. This is the most relevant category here.\n- **Whole-body (constitutional):** You have no fever, which fits a lower-tract pattern. Fever is the feature that would move this into a more serious category.\n- **Other possibilities to mention to a clinician:** The diabetes reference lists frequent urination as a possible early symptom. It is worth raising if you have thirst, weight loss, or tiredness. The other retrieved conditions (reflux, flu, common cold) do not fit this pattern well.\n\nA clinician can assess this and decide what, if anything, is needed. Book an appointment if your symptoms persist, worsen, or you are unsure.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
